golden.note:
`std::roundf` already takes a floating-point argument; casting `value` to `float` is fine but consider whether the intent is to avoid `long`->`float` narrowing for large `value` (precision loss). If exact percent conversion is required for large integers, prefer doing the division in integer/long double domain (e.g., `static_cast<float>(value) / 100.0f` without rounding, or use `std::round(static_cast<double>(value))` / `100.0`), depending on expected semantics.

```suggestion
    // If rounding to nearest integer percent is intended, keep but consider double for range:
    return static_cast<float>(std::round(static_cast<double>(value))) / 100.0F;
```
